WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.000
It is tempting to cast a buffer of bytes to a C struct and start reading fields.

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:11.060
That shortcut often looks tidy in a prototype, but it is a poor default for robust binary parsing.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:14.080
Raw bytes belong to the file or protocol format.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:21.180
C structs belong to the compiler, target architecture, ABI, alignment rules, and options used to build the program.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:25.160
A C struct may contain padding between fields or at the end.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:28.420
That padding is not necessarily present in the file format.

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:31.920
The compiler may align fields differently on different targets.

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:38.700
A packed struct option may reduce padding, but it can also create unaligned accesses and portability concerns.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:44.760
Even when a cast appears to work on one machine, it may encode assumptions that were never part of the format contract.

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:46.900
Unaligned access is another trap.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:49.760
Some platforms tolerate it with a performance cost.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:52.380
Others may fault or behave differently.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:59.400
A parser that depends on direct unaligned loads from arbitrary byte buffers is harder to port and harder to reason about.

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:03.060
The safer pattern is to decode packed data field by field.

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:11.840
Read the exact bytes required for each field, apply the format's byte order, check the resulting value, and only then store it in an internal representation.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:20.540
The internal representation can use normal C structs, but those structs represent trusted parser state after validation, not a direct view of untrusted bytes.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:22.920
Explicit decoding also helps review.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:30.980
A reviewer can see where the length was checked, where the integer width was chosen, where endianness was handled, and where unsupported values were rejected.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:35.680
That visibility is difficult to achieve when parsing is hidden behind a cast.
